x1,y1),(x2,y2),(x3,y3) such that they are collinear and slopes between any two are equal. Let's search manually.\n\nWe need points with distinct x and distinct sums. Let's list all points with coordinates:\n\n(1,1) sum2\n(1,2) sum3\n(1,3) sum4\n(1,4) sum5\n(2,1) sum3\n(2,2) sum4\n(2,3) sum5\n(3,1) sum4\n(3,2) sum5\n(4,1) sum5\n\nNow, find three points that are collinear with slope not 0,∞,-1.\n\nWe can try to find lines of slope 2: y = 2x + b. For integer points, we need y - 2x = b constant. Compute y - 2x for each point:\n\n(1,1): 1-2= -1\n(1,2): 2-2=0\n(1,3): 3-2=1\n(1,4): 4-2=2\n(2,1): 1-4= -3\n(2,2): 2-4= -2\n(2,3): 3-4= -1\n(3,1): 1-6= -5\n(3,2): 2-6= -4\n(4,1): 1-8= -7\n\nWe see that (1,1) and (2,3) both have y-2x = -1. So they lie on line y = 2x -1. That's slope 2, sunny. Also (3,5) would be on that line but not in set. So line L1: (1,1),(2,3). That's two points.\n\nNow slope 2 also includes (1,2) with y-2x=0, (2,4) not in set. So no other.\n\nNow slope 1/2: y = (1/2)x + b => 2y - x = constant. Compute 2y - x for each point:\n\n(1,1): 2-1=1\n(1,2): 4-1=3\n(1,3): 6-1=5\n(1,4): 8-1=7\n(2,1): 2-2=0\n(2,2): 4-2=2\n(2,3): 6-2=4\n(3,1): 2-3=-1\n(3,2): 4-3=1\n(4,1): 2-4=-2\n\nWe see (1,1) and (3,2) both have 2y - x = 1. So line y = (1/2)x + 0.5? Actually solving: 2y - x = 1 => y = (x+1)/2. For x=1, y=1; for x=3, y=2. So line L2: (1,1),(3,2). That's slope 1/2, sunny. Also (5,3) not in set.\n\nAlso (2,2) has 2y - x = 2, (4,3) not in set. So only those two.\n\nNow slope -2: y = -2x + b => y + 2x = b constant. Compute y + 2x:\n\n(1,1): 1+2=3\n(1,2): 2+2=4\n(1,3): 3+2=5\n(1,4): 4+2=6\n(2,1): 1+4=5\n(2,2): 2+4=6\n(2,3): 3+4=7\n(3,1): 1+6=7\n(3,2): 2+6=8\n(4,1): 1+8=9\n\nWe see (1,3) and (2,1) both have sum 5. So line L3: (1,3),(2,1). That's slope -2, sunny. Also (3,-1) not in set.\n\nAlso (1,4) and (2,2) both have sum 6: line L4: (1,4),(2,2). That's slope -2, sunny.\n\nAlso (2,3) and (3,1) both have sum 7: line L5: (2,3),(3,1). That's slope -2, sunny.\n\nAlso (3,2) and (4,? ) sum 8: (3,2) sum 8, (4,? ) would need y+2x=8 => y=8-8=0, not allowed. So only those pairs.\n\nThus we have many pairs of points that lie on sunny lines of slope -2.\n\nNow slope -1/2: y = -0.5x + b => 2y + x = constant. Compute 2y + x:\n\n(1,1): 2+1=3\n(1,2): 4+1=5\n(1,3): 6+1=7\n(1,4): 8+1=9\n(2,1): 2+2=4\n(2,2): 4+2=6\n(2,3): 6+2=8\n(3,1): 2+3=5\n(3,2): 4+3=7\n(4,1): 2+4=6\n\nWe see (1,1) and (2,? )? Actually (1,1) sum 3, (2,? ) with 2y + x = 3? (2,? ) would need 2y+2=3 => y=0.5, not integer. So no.\n\n(1,2) and (3,1) both have 5: line L6: (1,2),(3,1). That's slope -0.5, sunny.\n\n(1,3) and (3,2) both have 7: line L7: (1,3),(3,2). That's slope -0.5, sunny.\n\n(1,4) and (4,1) both have 9: line L8: (1,4),(4,1). That's slope -0.5, sunny.\n\n(2,1) and (1,? )? Already considered.\n\n(2,2) and (4,? )? (2,2) sum 6, (4,? ) with 2y+4=6 => y=1, so (4,1) also sum 6? Actually (4,1) sum 6, yes. So line L9: (2,2),(4,1). That's slope -0.5, sunny.\n\n(2,3) and (??) sum 8: (2,3) sum 8, (??) with 2y + x = 8 => maybe (3,? ) => 2y+3=8 => y=2.5, not integer. So no.\n\nThus many pairs.\n\nNow slope 1: y = x + b => y - x = constant. Compute y - x:\n\n(1,1): 0\n(1,2): 1\n(1,3): 2\n(1,4): 3\n(2,1): -1\n(2,2): 0\n(2,3): 1\n(3,1): -2\n(3,2): -1\n(4,1): -3\n\nWe see (1,1) and (2,2) both have y-x=0: line L10: (1,1),(2,2). That's slope 1, sunny.\n\n(1,2) and (2,3) both have y-x=1: line L11: (1,2),(2,3). That's slope 1, sunny.\n\n(1,3) and (2,4) not in set.\n\n(2,1) and (3,2) both have y-x=-1: line L12: (2,1),(3,2). That's slope 1, sunny.\n\n(3,1) and (4,2) not in set.\n\n(1,4) and (2,5) not in set.\n\n(3,1) and (2,? )? Already.\n\nThus we have many pairs.\n\nNow slope -1: not allowed.\n\nThus we have many possible pairs of points that can be covered by a sunny line.\n\nNow we need to cover all 10 points with 4 lines. Since each line can contain at most 2 points (maybe 3?), but we can try to use lines that contain 3 points. Let's see if any line contains 3 points.\n\nCheck slope -2 lines: we have pairs (1,3)-(2,1), (1,4)-(2,2), (2,3)-(3,1). Could any of these be extended to a third point? For slope -2, the equation is y = -2x + b. For (1,3), b = 5. So line y = -2x + 5 passes through (1,3),(2,1). Does it also pass through (3,-1) not allowed. So only two points.\n\nFor (1,4), b = 6, line y = -2x + 6 passes through (1,4),(2,2),(3,0) not allowed. So only two points.\n\nFor (2,3), b = 7, line y = -2x + 7 passes through (2,3),(3,1),(4,-1) not allowed. So only two points.\n\nThus slope -2 lines have at most 2 points.\n\nSlope -0.5 lines: equation y = -0.5x + b => 2y = -x + 2b => x + 2y = constant. For (1,2), constant = 1+4=5. So line x+2y=5 passes through (1,2),(3,1). Does it also pass through (5,0) not allowed. So only two points.\n\nFor (1,3), constant = 1+6=7, line x+2y=7 passes through (1,3),(3,2),(5,1) not allowed. So only two points.\n\nFor (1,4), constant = 1+8=9, line x+2y=9 passes through (1,4),(4,1),(7,? ) not allowed. So only two points.\n\nFor (2,2), constant = 2+4=6, line x+2y=6 passes through (2,2),(4,1),(6,0) not allowed. So only two points.\n\nThus slope -0.5 lines have at most 2 points.\n\nSlope 2 lines: y = 2x + b => y - 2x = b. For (1,1), b = -1. So line y = 2x -1 passes through (1,1),(2,3). Does it also pass through (3,5) not allowed. So only two points.\n\nFor (1,2), b = 0, line y = 2x passes through (1,2),(2,4) not in set. So only one point.\n\nThus slope 2 lines have at most 2 points.\n\nSlope 1/2 lines: y = 0.5x + b => 2y - x = constant. For (1,1), constant = 2-1=1. So line 2y - x = 1 passes through (1,1),(3,2). Does it also pass through (5,3) not in set. So only two points.\n\nThus any line seems to have at most 2 points from the set. So each line can cover at most 2 points. So with 4 lines we can cover at most 8 points, but we have 10 points. So it's impossible to cover all 10 points with 4 sunny lines. Therefore k=4 is impossible for n=4. So answer {0,1,3} might be correct for n=4.\n\nBut we need to verify for larger n if k≥4 is impossible. The solution's argument \"sunny lines cover at most one point per column\" is insufficient, but maybe there is a stronger argument that for any n≥3, k≥4 is impossible. Let's try to see if we can find a construction for n=5 with k=4. If we can find a construction, answer {0,1,3} would be wrong. If we cannot, answer might be correct.\n\nWe need to examine the general problem. Let's try to reason about the maximum possible number of sunny lines.\n\nWe have n lines total. The non-sunny lines are vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. Let's denote V = number of vertical lines, H = number of horizontal lines, D = number of slope -1 lines. So V+H+D = n - k (non-sunny lines). The vertical lines can each cover an entire column. Horizontal lines can each cover an entire row (but rows are not defined in the problem; we have points with b coordinate, but there is no bound on b beyond b ≤ n+1-a. So horizontal lines can cover points across columns but only one per column. Similarly, slope -1 lines can cover points across columns but only one per column.\n\nThus vertical lines are the only non-sunny lines that can cover multiple points in a column. So to cover columns with many points, we need vertical lines. The column sizes are decreasing from n to 1. So we need at least n - (n - k) = k columns that are not covered by vertical lines (since we have at most n - k vertical lines). Actually we have at most n - k non-sunny lines, but we could allocate all of them as vertical lines. So we can have at most n - k vertical lines. So we can fully cover at most n - k columns (by vertical lines). The remaining k columns must be covered by sunny lines (or horizontal or slope -1 lines). But we have exactly k sunny lines, and we could also have horizontal or slope -1 lines among the non-sunny lines, but those would not help covering multiple points in a column. So the best we can do is to use all non-sunny lines as vertical lines, covering n - k columns fully. The remaining k columns must be covered by the k sunny lines (since we have exactly k sunny lines). Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so each column can be covered by at most k points (one per sunny line). But each column has size equal to its column index? Actually column i has n+1-i points. So for the remaining columns, we need to ensure that each column's size ≤ k (since we have only k sunny lines to cover them). So we need to choose the k columns with smallest sizes (i.e., columns n-k+1,...,n). The largest size among those is column n-k+1, which has n+1-(n-k+1) = k points. So each of those columns has at most k points. So it's possible to cover them with k sunny lines (each line covering one point per column). So the column constraint does not rule out k≥4.\n\nNow consider anti-diagonals (sums). The same reasoning applies: each non-sunny line can be a slope -1 line, which can cover multiple points on a given anti-diagonal (since slope -1 lines are exactly the anti-diagonals). So we could use D slope -1 lines to cover some anti-diagonals fully. However, we have only n - k non-sunny lines total, which we might allocate partly to vertical lines and partly to slope -1 lines. The anti-diagonal sizes are also decreasing from n to 1 (sum s has s-1 points). So we could cover some anti-diagonals fully with slope -1 lines. The remaining anti-diagonals must be covered by sunny lines (or horizontal lines). Horizontal lines can cover at most one point per anti-diagonal (since they intersect each anti-diagonal at most one point). So the same constraints apply.\n\nThus the problem reduces to covering the triangular grid with n lines, exactly k of which are sunny. The non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. We need to see which k are possible.\n\nThe solution claims only k=0,1,3 are possible. Let's test for small n values to see if any other k works.\n\nWe can brute force for small n (like n=3,4,5) to see possible k. But we need to reason manually.\n\nFor n=3, we have 6 points. We need 3 lines. Let's enumerate possible k values.\n\n- k=0: all lines non-sunny. We can use vertical lines x=1,2,3. That covers all points. So k=0 possible.\n\n- k=1: one sunny line, two non-sunny lines. We can use vertical lines x=1,2 (non-sunny) and a sunny line covering remaining points (maybe (3,1) and (2,2) and (1,3) etc). Let's try: vertical lines x=1 (covers (1,1),(1,2),(1,3)), x=2 (covers (2,1),(2,2)). Then remaining point (3,1) must be covered by a sunny line. That's fine. So k=1 possible.\n\n- k=2: two sunny lines, one non-sunny line. Non-sunny line can be vertical x=1 (covers (1,1),(1,2),(1,3)). Then we have points (2,1),(2,2),(3,1) left. We need to cover them with two sunny lines. As we saw earlier, any line covering (2,1) and (3,1) would be horizontal (y=1) not allowed. Any line covering (2,1) and (2,2) would be vertical (x=2) not allowed. Any line covering (2,2) and (3,1) slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. So each sunny line can cover at most one of these three points. So we need at least three sunny lines. So k=2 impossible. So k=2 not possible.\n\n- k=3: all three lines sunny. We need to cover all six points with three sunny lines. As we found earlier for n=3, we can pair points: L1: (1,1)-(2,2) slope 1; L2: (1,2)-(3,1) slope -0.5; L3: (1,3)-(2,1) slope -2. That covers all six points. So k=3 possible.\n\nThus for n=3, possible k are {0,1,3}. So matches answer.\n\nNow for n=4, we have 10 points, need 4 lines.\n\n- k=0: all non-sunny lines. Use vertical lines x=1,2,3,4. That covers all points. So k=0 possible.\n\n- k=1: one sunny line, three non-sunny lines. Use vertical lines x=1,2,3 (non-sunny) covering columns 1-3 fully. Remaining column 4 has point (4,1). Use sunny line to cover (4,1). So k=1 possible.\n\n- k=2: two sunny lines, two non-sunny lines. Use vertical lines x=1,2 (non-sunny) covering columns 1-2 fully. Remaining columns 3 (points (3,1),(3,2)) and 4 (point (4,1)). We have two sunny lines to cover these three points. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So we need at least three sunny lines to cover three points across two columns? Actually we have three points: (3,1),(3,2),(4,1). We have two sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most one point in column 3, and at most one point in column 4. So with two sunny lines, we can cover at most 2 points in column 3 (one per line) and at most 2 points in column 4 (but column 4 has only 1 point). So we can cover at most 2+1 = 3 points? Actually each line can cover at most one point in column 3, so two lines can cover at most 2 points in column 3. Column 3 has 2 points, so that's okay. Column 4 has 1 point, can be covered by one of the lines. So total capacity is enough. However, we need to ensure that the points assigned to each line are collinear (sunny). Let's try to assign: we need to cover (3,1),(3,2),(4,1) with two sunny lines. Let's try to pair (3,1) with (4,1) on a line: slope = (1-1)/(4-3) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. So cannot pair those. Pair (3,1) with (3,2) vertical, not allowed. Pair (3,2) with (4,1): slope = (1-2)/(4-3) = -1, not allowed. So any pair among these three points yields a non-sunny line. So each sunny line can cover at most one of these points. So we need three sunny lines to cover three points. So k=2 impossible.\n\nThus k=2 impossible for n=4.\n\n- k=3: three sunny lines, one non-sunny line. Use vertical line x=1 (non-sunny) covering column 1 (points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4)). Remaining points: columns 2 (3 points), 3 (2 points), 4 (1 point) = total 6 points. We have three sunny lines to cover 6 points. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so across columns 2,3,4, each line can cover at most 3 points (one per column). So three lines can cover at most 9 points, enough. But we need to assign points to lines such that each line's points are collinear. Let's try to find a construction.\n\nWe need to cover points: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). We have three sunny lines. Let's try to pair them as we did for n=3 but extended.\n\nWe can try to use lines of slope -2: (2,3)-(3,1) slope -2, (2,2)-(3,? )? Actually (2,2)-(3,? ) slope -? Let's systematically find pairs.\n\nList all possible sunny lines (pairs) among these points:\n\nCompute slopes for each pair:\n\n- (2,1)-(3,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n- (2,1)-(4,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n- (2,1)-(3,2): slope = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, allowed.\n- (2,1)-(2,2): vertical, not allowed.\n- (2,1)-(2,3): vertical, not allowed.\n- (2,2)-(3,1): slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed.\n- (2,2)-(3,2): horizontal, not allowed.\n- (2,2)-(4,1): slope = (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2, allowed.\n- (2,2)-(2,3): vertical, not allowed.\n- (2,3)-(3,1): slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, allowed.\n- (2,3)-(3,2): slope = (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed.\n- (2,3)-(4,1): slope = (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n- (3,1)-(3,2): vertical, not allowed.\n- (3,1)-(4,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n- (3,2)-(4,1): slope = (1-2)/(4-3) = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus the allowed pairs (sunny) are:\n\n- (2,1)-(3,2) slope 1\n- (2,2)-(4,1) slope -0.5\n- (2,3)-(3,1) slope -2\n\nThus we have three disjoint pairs covering all six points! Indeed, we have exactly three pairs that are sunny lines. So we can assign each pair to a sunny line. So we have three sunny lines covering all remaining points. So k=3 is possible for n=4.\n\nThus for n=4, possible k are {0,1,3}. So matches answer.\n\nNow for n=5, we have 15 points, need 5 lines.\n\nWe can test possible k values.\n\n- k=0: use vertical lines x=1..5, works.\n\n- k=1: use vertical lines x=1..4, sunny line for column 5 point (5,1). Works.\n\n- k=2: use vertical lines x=1..3 (non-sunny), leaving columns 4 (points (4,1),(4,2)), column 5 (point (5,1)). That's total 3 points left. We have 2 sunny lines to cover them. As before, we need to see if any pair among these three points yields a sunny line. Points: (4,1),(4,2),(5,1). Pairs:\n\n(4,1)-(4,2): vertical, not allowed.\n(4,1)-(5,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n(4,2)-(5,1): slope = (1-2)/(5-4) = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus any pair is non-sunny. So each sunny line can cover at most one point, so we need at least 3 sunny lines. So k=2 impossible.\n\n- k=3: use vertical lines x=1..2 (non-sunny), leaving columns 3 (points (3,1),(3,2),(3,3)), column 4 (points (4,1),(4,2)), column 5 (point (5,1)). That's total 6 points left. We have 3 sunny lines to cover them. We need to see if we can pair them into 3 sunny lines covering all 6 points. Let's list points:\n\nC3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3)\nC4: (4,1),(4,2)\nC5: (5,1)\n\nWe need to partition into 3 pairs (or maybe one line could cover 3 points). Let's try to find sunny pairs.\n\nCompute slopes for pairs across columns:\n\n- (3,1)-(4,2): slope = (2-1)/(4-3) = 1, allowed.\n- (3,1)-(5,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n- (3,1)-(4,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n- (3,2)-(4,1): slope = (1-2)/(4-3) = -1, not allowed.\n- (3,2)-(4,2): vertical, not allowed.\n- (3,2)-(5,1): slope = (1-2)/(5-3) = -1/2, allowed.\n- (3,3)-(4,1): slope = (1-3)/(4-3) = -2, allowed.\n- (3,3)-(4,2): slope = (2-3)/(4-3) = -1, not allowed.\n- (3,3)-(5,1): slope = (1-3)/(5-3) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n- (4,1)-(5,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n- (4,2)-(5,1): slope = (1-2)/(5-4) = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus allowed pairs are:\n\n- (3,1)-(4,2) slope 1\n- (3,2)-(5,1) slope -0.5\n- (3,3)-(4,1) slope -2\n\nThese three pairs are disjoint and cover all six points! Indeed, (3,1) with (4,2), (3,2) with (5,1), (3,3) with (4,1). So we have three sunny lines covering all points. So k=3 possible for n=5.\n\n- k=4: use vertical lines x=1 (non-sunny), leaving columns 2 (points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4)), column 3 (3 points), column 4 (2 points), column 5 (1 point). That's total points left: column 2 has 4 points, column 3 has 3, column 4 has 2, column 5 has 1 = total 10 points. We have 4 sunny lines to cover 10 points. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so across columns 2-5 (4 columns), each line can cover at most 4 points. So 4